/*Q2. Aanya is working on a body mass index (BMI) calculator. Write a Java program that prompts the user to enter their weight in kilograms and
height in meters. Handle the exception if any of the inputs cannot be parsed into a double. Calculate the BMI and print the result. If any
input is invalid, print "Error: Invalid input".
[BMI = Weight / (Height × Height)]

Input Format:

• The first line contains a string representing the weight in kilograms. It must be convertible to a valid double value.

• The second line contains a string representing the height in meters. It must be convertible to a valid double value.
Output Format:

• If both inputs can be successfully converted into double values, calculate the BMI.

• Print the calculated BMI rounded to exactly two decimal places.

• If either input cannot be converted into a double value, print:

Error: Invalid input
Note: The height must be non-zero to calculate a finite BMI value. If the height is zero, treat it as invalid input and print:
Error: Invalid input
*/
import java.util.Scanner;
 
public class BMICalculator {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        try {
            String w = sc.hasNextLine() ? sc.nextLine().trim() : "";
            String h = sc.hasNextLine() ? sc.nextLine().trim() : "";
            double weight = Double.parseDouble(w);
            double height = Double.parseDouble(h);
            if (height == 0) {
                throw new ArithmeticException("Height cannot be zero");
            }
            double bmi = weight / (height * height);
            if (Double.isNaN(bmi) || Double.isInfinite(bmi)) {
                throw new ArithmeticException("Invalid BMI");
            }
            System.out.printf("%.2f%n", bmi);
        } catch (NumberFormatException | ArithmeticException e) {
            System.out.println("Error: Invalid input");
        }
        sc.close();
    }
}
